 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T19:07+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, you should strongly consider staying indoors in Kuala Lumpur today.** \n\nCurrent air quality readings in parts of Kuala Lumpur register at **unhealthy** levels. Fine particulate matter ($PM_{2.5}$) and other pollutants are high, which can easily trigger asthma attacks, chest tightness, coughing, and shortness of breath. \n\n### Recommendations for Today:\n* **Limit Outdoor Exposure:** Avoid unnecessary trips outside, and completely skip any outdoor exercise or strenuous physical activity. \n* **Keep Indoor Air Clean:** Keep your windows and doors closed to prevent polluted air and haze from entering your living space. If you have an air purifier with a HEPA filter, run it indoors.\n* **Have Your Medication Ready:** Ensure your quick-relief inhaler (rescue inhaler) is close by at all times. Follow your personal asthma action plan meticulously. \n* **Wear a Mask If You Must Go Out:** If you absolutely have to step outside, wear a protective mask (such as an N95 or KN95 respirator) rather than a loose cloth or surgical mask, as standard masks do not filter out fine pollutant particles effectively.\n\nMonitor the official Department of Environment (DOE) APIMS updates or your local weather/air quality app for changes throughout the day. If you begin experiencing persistent wheezing, chest tightness, or shortness of breath that does not improve with your rescue inhaler, seek medical help immediately.",
